    <h1>About China AI Hub</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Last updated: 2026-10-23</p>

    <h2>What China AI Hub is</h2>
    <p>
      China AI Hub is an independent, English-language knowledge and intelligence platform for
      understanding China's AI ecosystem — models, agents, APIs, companies, technologies, pricing,
      benchmarks and the relationships between them. It exists to answer one practical question:
      <em>which Chinese AI model, agent or API is suitable for which task, at what cost, with what
      capabilities, and under what deployment conditions?</em>
    </p>
    <p>Concretely, the site is four things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>database</strong> of models, companies, agents, APIs, pricing and benchmarks;</li>
      <li>A <strong>knowledge base</strong> of AI technologies and their Chinese implementations;</li>
      <li>A <strong>comparison system</strong> that explains trade-offs rather than declaring winners;</li>
      <li>A <strong>research platform</strong> publishing original analysis with clearly labeled evidence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What China AI Hub is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not an official source — the site is <strong>not affiliated with, endorsed by, or operated by any AI vendor</strong> (DeepSeek, Alibaba Cloud, ByteDance, Moonshot, MiniMax, Zhipu or others).</li>
      <li>Not a news aggregator or translation site.</li>
      <li>Not a "Top 10 Chinese AI models" listicle.</li>
      <li>Not a ranking site that declares one best model.</li>
      <li>Not a collection of rewritten official documentation.</li>
      <li>Not a provider of AI systems, APIs, or any paid service.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why this website exists</h2>
    <p>
      China's AI ecosystem moves quickly and its documentation is fragmented across Chinese-language
      sources. International developers and researchers routinely lack a reliable, structured,
      English-language view of what exists, what it costs, and what evidence supports it. China AI
      Hub fills that gap with structured data and sourced analysis — not with hype or rankings.
      The full scope and limits of what the content can be relied on for are in the
      <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What is on the site</h2>
    <p>
      The site is organized around eleven content collections, each answering a different question:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/models/">Models</a> — Chinese AI models and their specifications, licenses and benchmarks.</li>
      <li><a href="/companies/">Companies</a> — the companies behind the models, and their products.</li>
      <li><a href="/agents/">Agents</a> — Chinese coding and general-purpose AI agents.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/">API</a> — the API platforms and their endpoints, compatibility and capabilities.</li>
      <li><a href="/pricing/">Pricing</a> — normalized API pricing across providers.</li>
      <li><a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a> — benchmark evaluations and their methodology.</li>
      <li><a href="/technology/">Technology</a> — a knowledge base of AI technologies with Chinese adoption context.</li>
      <li><a href="/comparisons/">Comparisons</a> — evidence-based model comparisons, without rankings.</li>
      <li><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> — decision frameworks for choosing and evaluating models.</li>
      <li><a href="/research/">Research</a> — original analysis with labeled evidence.</li>
      <li><a href="/news/">News</a> — short, sourced release notes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each entity page (models, companies, agents, APIs, pricing, benchmarks) is a data record with
      sourced fields and a verification status; the editorial pages (research, guides, comparisons,
      technology, news) add interpretation on top of that data. The same facts are also served in
      machine-readable form on the companion Data Hub (<code>data.sinoaihub.com</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>Who it serves</h2>
    <p>
      AI developers, software engineers, researchers, enterprise technology buyers, startups,
      analysts and journalists — anyone evaluating Chinese AI systems from outside China. Content
      assumes an intelligent reader who knows AI but may not know China's AI ecosystem.
    </p>

    <h2>Who operates and writes the site</h2>
    <p>
      The site is operated by the China AI Hub project. Research and writing are done by the
      <a href="/authors/">SinoAI Hub Research Team</a> — a team-level byline, not a set of named
      individuals. The project deliberately avoids publishing invented individual "expert" profiles;
      accountability comes from the source layer (every claim carries a source, a source type and a
      verification date), not from a persona. The project is independent and ad-free, as described
      in the <a href="/disclosure/">funding disclosure</a>. The project does not publish a corporate
      legal name, registration number, or registered office; the contact point is
      Martin.W — <a href="mailto:admin@sinoaihub.com">admin@sinoaihub.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How data is sourced</h2>
    <p>
      Every important factual claim carries a source, a source type, a publication date and a
      last-verified date. Sources are ranked in a four-tier hierarchy — primary sources first
      (official documentation, model cards, pricing pages, papers, GitHub), then high-quality
      technical sources, then major media, then community — and a community-only source can never
      carry a critical fact alone. Where sources conflict, the conflict is explained, not silently
      resolved. Where data is unknown, the site says so. Nothing is invented.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full collection hierarchy, the verification workflow, and the eight-field evidence layer
      are documented on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page. How the site is licensed,
      how the main site and the Data Hub (<code>data.sinoaihub.com</code>) divide labor, and how
      often data is re-verified are on the <a href="/data-policy/">Data Policy</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>Verification and evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Every entity record carries a verification status: <code>verified</code> or
      <code>partially_verified</code>. A record is <code>partially_verified</code> when a core
      field is documented as not publicly disclosed — the gap is stated, never hidden. Across the
      reference dataset this is currently a <strong>45 / 14 split</strong> (45 records fully
      verified, 14 partially verified), and the evidence layer normalizes every source into eight
      fields — identifier, name, URL, type, publication date, verification date, confidence, and
      conflict flag. This is what makes the site's claims checkable rather than asserted.
    </p>

    <h2>Analysis policy</h2>
    <p>
      Editorial analysis (including the sections labeled "China AI Hub analysis") is always labeled
      as analysis and is traceable to cited evidence. The site does not promote or attack companies,
      does not declare universal winners, and does not pretend certainty where none exists. The four
      claim labels — Official fact, Vendor-reported claim, Third-party evidence, and China AI Hub
      analysis — are applied throughout so the reader can always tell whose claim they are reading.
      The quality gate is documented in the <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Use of AI in producing the site</h2>
    <p>
      The site is transparent about where AI-assisted tooling is used. Illustrations on entity pages
      are <strong>AI-generated</strong> and labeled in image credits (e.g. "Seedream"). Automated
      tooling is used in the <a href="/methodology/">verification workflow</a> to re-fetch primary
      sources and compare extracted values against stored values. Editorial text and analysis are
      produced and reviewed by the SinoAI Hub Research Team, and analysis is labeled. See the
      <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> for the complete AI disclosure.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read the site</h2>
    <p>
      The site is built so you can tell, on any page, what kind of statement you are reading. Entity
      pages open with a short answer block that states what the thing is, why it matters, its key
      characteristics, and what a professional should know, before the detailed data. Facts carry
      sources and verification dates; analysis is labeled. Every record shows a verification status
      (<code>verified</code> or <code>partially_verified</code>), and the four claim labels —
      Official fact, Vendor-reported claim, Third-party evidence, and China AI Hub analysis — tell
      you whose claim you are reading.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comparisons explain trade-offs without declaring a winner; decision guides route you to the
      best-documented fit for a scenario rather than to a "best model"; research pages state their
      method and their uncertainties. If a value is unknown, the page says so rather than guessing.
      The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial
      standards</a> pages define these conventions in full.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial values</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero fabrication</strong> — no invented prices, benchmarks, parameters, dates or company details. Unknown values are marked, not guessed.</li>
      <li><strong>No winner verdicts</strong> — the site explains trade-offs and suitability rather than declaring a universal best model.</li>
      <li><strong>Honest uncertainty</strong> — where evidence is thin or conflicting, the site says so, and does not pretend certainty.</li>
      <li><strong>Source over persona</strong> — credibility comes from the evidence layer, not from invented author authority.</li>
      <li><strong>Verification over speed</strong> — a sourced, date-stamped record beats a fast, unsourced claim.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Independence and funding</h2>
    <p>
      The site operates with no advertising, no sponsored listings, no paid placements and no
      affiliate links. No AI vendor pays for placement, ranking or description. The project is
      operated at its own expense and currently has no revenue model; if that changes, the
      <a href="/disclosure/">funding disclosure</a> will be updated before the change takes effect.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and accountability</h2>
    <p>
      Errors are handled through a public, recorded process — see the
      <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a> — and site changes are logged in the
      <a href="/updates/">update history</a>. Data licensing and reuse terms are in the
      <a href="/data-policy/">Data Policy</a> and the <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How the main site and the Data Hub relate</h2>
    <p>
      The project deliberately splits content across two properties so that analysis and raw data
      stay separable. The main site (<code>sinoaihub.com</code>) is the editorial authority: it
      explains what things mean, why they matter, and how to choose between them. The Data Hub
      (<code>data.sinoaihub.com</code>) is the reference layer: it carries the exact, machine-readable
      facts and their evidence records. Every Data Hub record links back to its canonical main-site
      page, and the two layers are kept in agreement by automated cross-checks. Interpretation lives
      on the main site; the Data Hub is not a second blog.
    </p>

    <h2>How the site stays current</h2>
    <p>
      Data changes on three cadences, and staleness is shown rather than hidden: light weekly
      re-checks of pricing and status for top models and providers; a monthly full crawl of all
      monitored targets; and event-driven updates within 24 hours of a major release, price cut or
      license change. Every record shows a <code>last_verified</code> date — under 30 days counts as
      current, 30–90 days is flagged "may be outdated", and over 90 days is marked prominently for
      verification before reliance. The full schedule is in the
      <a href="/data-policy/">Data Policy</a>, and the chronological record of changes is in the
      <a href="/updates/">update history</a>.
    </p>

